     Alright, I just lost my second-best game do a cockatrice (my best game was
the Barbarian who never encountered an altar, shop, or sink on the first ten
levels)-- *to* a cockatrice, rather-- because I forgot to re-wield my
Snickersnee before attacking him (I was cleaning up the level in anticipation
of finding a polymorph wand somewhere).  That's all right-- I didn't res-
surect him out of my backup save file (for crashes).  But 1) I had a lizard
corpse.  Shouldn't it go through the same sequence as it does when I hear
its hissing, and give me a chance to save myself?  And 2) what was a
cockatrice doing on level THREE?  Incidentally, that was the second one I
had encountered on that game, the first being on level 1q2.
     14th level Samurai, BTW.  I notice a general increase in the difficulty
of monsters on low levels as experience goes up.  Why is this?  It's not par-
ticularly playable, and it's not particularly realistic either.  I've had
level six and seven players fall prey to killer bee swarms while resting on
dlvl 1, having done nothing to lower my luck (this particular game I had
robbed a shop, but about 12,000 moves back).  Generally I'm on dlvl 1 because
I'm resting for an attack on something down on dlvl 2 or 3 that drove me
back.  I see no reason why monsters on level 1 should get tougher as my char-
acter gets more experienced.
     I think the game is tough enough as it is, without all these drives to
push the character deeper than he can go (dungeon "entropy" a la rock mole,
lack of food [although you can live on faith, which is helpful], toughening
monsters, lack of useful equipment after a goblin, dwarf and Uruk-hai orc
have been killed, etc.).  While some of these are inherent, I think it makes
it significantly tougher for, say, a mage.
     In this case it really wasn't a problem, actually, except that I didn't
expect a cockatrice on that level (I still shouldn't have slipped, tech-
nically-- it wasn't an accidental keystroke; I simply forgot that I was 
weaponless at the time).

     BTW, that game I also lost eight AC points to a wand of cancellation.  I
now know that there are *three* wands which make engravings disappear when
enscribed in the ground (I was testing it out on myself to see whether it was
teleport away or make invisible) over a handwritten engraving.  -sigh-.

     On the brighter side, I found that repeated applications of a unicorn
horn will bring back strength points lost to poison.  This makes it signif-
icantly easier to get poison resistance (from killer bees), although in this
particular game I already had it from something or another.

